      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
Your house is reasonably air tight, with a few air leakage areas that will be addressed as you complete your Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program upgrades. The current air leakage locations include:

** The chimney for the current furnace (that will be eliminated when you upgrade the furnace)

** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)

** The basement header space and those "seasonal vents" through the foundation walls

** The current windows &amp; doors are generally well sealed, however the front door has air leakage along the bottom &amp; some of the 1996 windows no longer close tightly.

Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 72 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.2 GJ. For your house, you may even achieve twice this amount - however being too tight is not good, and I want you to read my Ventilation Sections of this report.

There are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction, unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate - achieving a 20% reduction increases the rebate to $750.

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x4 (R12). In conjunction with an exterior siding retrofit, I have modeled the addition of a minimum R20 (eg. 4" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the exterior of the main floor walls.

Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).

Savings:
By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R20, you could potentially save $ 841 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 26.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP),

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>
The attic space of your house has the original R20 batt insulation on the bottom, with a small layer of vermiculite insulation that was added on top afterwards (likely the early 80's). Sometime later (likely the early 90's), there was some blown cellulose insulation added on top. The total depth is around 8" - 9" which is about R28, and I have modeled an increase up to a minimum R50.

NOTE: Please ensure your insulation contractor is aware of the vermiculite insulation &amp; follows proper Health Canada safety protocol. If there are any projects to complete in the attic (wiring for a new light or ceiling fan), these should be completed before more insulation is added, and again following vermiculite safety protocol. Once all work in the attic is done, and new insulation has been added, the attic hatch can be sealed with a "removable caulking" such as Draft Attack. If you decide not to add any further attic insulation, it is a good idea to seal the attic hatch now with the removable caulking.

Savings: By increasing the attic insulation from R28 up to a minimum R50, you could potentially save $ 246 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the DHERP.

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral />
