                <foundation>
Your house has a shallow basement, that has ventilation openings above grade to ventilate any moisture that may accumulate on the floor during the late spring &amp; summer, with the vents being closed for the heating season, The basement has concrete walls that are insulated on the inside with 3" (R12) of semi rigid Roxul Insulation &amp; the header space above has very little insulation (almost none), and it appears the header space is a source of air leakage heat loss.

In conjunction with your exterior wall insulation upgrade, that additional R20 (and the new siding) will extend over the outside of the basement header space. I think you may want to insulate the inside of the header space as well (perhaps with R20 of spray foam insulation). You should research this further with qualified insulation contractors.

Savings: By completing the above insulation upgrades to the basement header space, you could potentially save $ 441 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the DHERP.

