                <windows>
You house has 8 windows, and 7 of them appear to have been updated in 1996, while the 8th one (in the laundry room) is a bit newer. In conjunction with your planned wall insulation upgrade (and thicker walls), this would be a good time to upgrade all of the windows with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified window rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend a full frame replacement window that is a triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.

Savings: 
By upgrading these 8 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 205 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.3 GJ, and qualify for a $ 250 rebate per window (8 windows maximum rebate = $2,000) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</windows>
                <doors>
Your house has a front wood door and a rear steel insulated door. The front door does not seal very well and has a low insulation value. You should consider upgrading the front door to an Energy Star insulated unit with a minimum insulation value of R6.

Savings: 
By upgrading the front wood door to an Energy Star insulated unit (minimum R6), you could potentially save $ 77 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates for replacing existing exterior doors. </doors>
                <VentilationSystem>
As outlined in the Air Sealing section of this report, your house is reasonably air tight and is expected to become tighter in conjunction with your planned renovations. If your house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed.

Almost all houses from the late 60's &amp; 70's would not have had an HRV and instead would manage fresh air supply &amp; indoor air quality by using exhaust fans (kitchen, bathroom, clothes dryer) and incorporate a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the furnace - that would allow outside air each time the furnace operates or when an exhaust fan is operating.

As you "Renovate Tight", you will also want to ensure you "Ventilate Right", which means you may want to consider installing an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) that is a minimum 75% efficient in transferring heat from the outgoing air to the incoming fresh air. The furnace can still be used to circulate the incoming "pre-heated" air throughout the house.

I'm not sure how easily an HRV could be retrofitted into your house, so you will need to rely on your local heating &amp; ventilation contractor to design &amp; install a ventilation system that keeps your home healthy &amp; comfortable. It may just be they will indicate a system, such as the one I outlined in the 2nd paragraph above, will be the best solution.

Please look at my Ventilation section in the "Additional Comments" of this report.

Savings: Installing a 75% efficient HRV is estimated to increase energy costs by $ 132 per year, however most or all of this will be offset by the savings from the reduced air leakage (see that section of my report).</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>
You currently have a 40 USG electric hot water tank. We discussed an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an Uniform Energy Factor of 0.91 or higher.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.91 UEF, could potentially save $ 1,135 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with an 82.1% efficient oil furnace that appears to be near its' "end of life". I have modeled the energy savings you could achieve if it was replaced with a new high-efficiency condensing gas furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 1469 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 26.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
 
To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
