                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>
Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $16,500 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:

     - $8,000 for the wall insulation
     - $2,000 for 8 main floor windows
     - $   500 for the ceiling insulation
     - $   500 for the basement header insulation
     - $3,000 for the heating system
     - $   500 for a 10% air leakage reduction ($7500 or $1,000 if you achieve a 20% or 30% reduction)
     - $2,000 for the bonus rebate (perhaps a $3,000 bonus rebate if you exceed a 40% reduction it total energy use)

NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the heating system rebate and the basement header + ceiling insulation, however these rebates would only be 50% of what is listed above.

In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:
     - On-Demand Hot Water (minimum 0.91 UEF) - $750
     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175
     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175
     - LED Lighting - see next section.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range "downdraft" fan, 1 bathroom fan &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 5.1 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace vents up a chimney (ie: not "direct vent", so it is susceptible to back drafting).

I noticed there is not an outside combustion air supply pipe for the current furnace, however the need for one will be eliminated if you complete the furnace upgrade outlined in this report.

During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has no exhaust ventilation:
        - The kitchen range "downdraft" fan
        - The main bathroom fan
        - The clothes dryer

I noticed the bathroom fan and the clothes dryer each have greater exhaust flow than the kitchen range "downdraft" fan ........... which surprised me as those downdraft fans usually have good flow - perhaps blocked or just older &amp; weak now.

Overall, your house is relatively air tight and will become tighter after a Deep Home Energy Retrofit and a new furnce is installed (that eliminates the current chimney).

One of the biggest challenges for new home builders and existing home owners is to balance air tightness with adequate ventilation. Each house &amp; the occupants requires a certain amount of fresh air in order to maintain a good indoor air quality, without over-ventilating &amp; wasting heat.

If your house was being built new today, it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit installed. These are harder to retrofit into older houses, so a more common way to add ventilation is to have an outside fresh air supply pipe directly connected to the return air plenum of your furnace, and include a "manual damper" that you can adjust to regulate the amount of fresh air the house receives each time:
     - the furnace operates
     - an exhaust fan (bathroom fan or dryer) operates

Some houses will add a timer switch to the main bathroom exhaust fan so that it will operate more frequently and draw in some outside fresh air each time it does.

You will know if your house is too tight in fall &amp; winter if there is excessive condensation building up on the windows or if odours (cooking or other) linger for too long &amp; the house starts to smell a bit "stale". If so, operate the exhaust fan &amp; open the fresh air supply damper a bit more.

You should consider upgrading to an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom exhaust fans that use little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

